<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>
  <!--
    The cooling system of a liquid-cooled combustion engine: the engine heats its coolant in proportion to the fuel it burns, the
    thermostat opens the radiator to the air, and a slow soak cools everything toward the air. Design:
    docs/development/vehicle-systems-design.md. Not yet tested in game. The integrator and these exact figures are checked offline
    (tests/PhobosFramework.Vehicles.Rules.Tests, CoolingFigureTests): change both together.

    Sources and labels:
    - Thermostats begin to open at about 82 to 90 degrees C. Secondary: engine handbooks quoted on owners' forums; a Detroit Diesel
      thermostat listed at 87 degrees C. We use 88 with an 8 degree band (authored).
    - A warm engine's coolant runs at about 85 to 105 degrees C, and pressurised systems run above 100. Secondary: Wikipedia,
      "Internal combustion engine cooling".
    - Overheating damages the engine's materials and lubricant; boiling coolant causes a sudden loss of cooling, and a failing
      head gasket feeds back into more heat. Secondary: Wikipedia, "Internal combustion engine cooling" and "Head gasket". The
      floors of 110, 120 and 130 degrees C are authored for a pressurised system.
    - Cold running wears an engine faster; cold starts are a large share of all wear. Secondary: a Szczecin Maritime University study
      of start-up wear; SAE 2006-01-3413 as cited second-hand (unverified). The wear figures are authored and small.
    - Heat at full load (420 degrees an hour), the radiator's rate (6 an hour), the soak (0.6 an hour) and the slower radiator when
      stopped (0.7) are authored so an engine warms in about a quarter hour and settles at 88 to 110 degrees C.
    - With a drivetrain (docs/development/vehicle-drivetrain-design.md), the heat follows the fuel the model burns: about 28% of the
      fuel's energy goes into the coolant (secondary: the record's energy balance), as a share of the heat at the engine's rated
      power, so 420 degrees an hour still means full load. Without one, it follows the simple engine load as before.
    - The radiator's fan and water pump turn with the engine, so a stopped or idling engine cools less (authored curve on engine
      speed: 60% stopped, 75% at idle, full from 1,500 rpm).
    - A coolant top-up is its own service, due once the coolant is down to 60%. With Dubs Bad Hygiene the coolant is water from
      plumbing beside the vehicle; without it, chemfuel stands in for the coolant, as it does for oil (owner, 8 Nov 2026; the amounts
      are authored).
    - Room heat: the engine's waste heat goes into the room it stands in, 12 heat units a second at full load (authored; a
      vanilla heater gives about 21), instead of Vehicle Framework's flat ambient heat.
    - The night heater warms the engine as well as the cab (crosslink 1, Framework 0.67.0; owner, 10 Nov 2026: every vehicle with
      cooling). A block or coolant heater keeps coolant and oil warm overnight, so the engine starts easily and wears less (secondary:
      Wikipedia, "Block heater"). It pulls the coolant toward 40 degrees C at 2 an hour against the soak's 0.6 (authored), holding it
      near 26 at -20 outside and near 21 at -40: above the 20 degrees below which a start wears the engine. The oil follows the coolant
      (Systems_Lubrication.xml), so it warms too. With a closed cab the cab system burns the heater's fuel; on an open vehicle this
      system does, so the heater burns once either way. An air-cooled engine has no coolant here and is not warmed.
    - A worn engine runs hot (crosslink 3, Framework 0.68.0): blow-by and poor combustion in a tired engine put more of the fuel's heat
      into the coolant (secondary, approximate; no figure checked). A fifth more heat at 40% health and below (authored). The record
      proposed twice as much, but at full load the radiator holds the coolant near 70 degrees above the air, so double heat would boil
      any worn engine over in the first warm day and wear it further, a spiral rather than a sign; a fifth more takes a hard-worked
      worn engine into "running hot" on a 35 degree day, which the player can see and act on (a quarter reached "overheating").
      CoolingFigureTests hold these figures.
    - A burning vehicle cooks its cooling (crosslink 4, Framework 0.69.0; owner, 10 Nov 2026: a fire put out within an in-game hour
      leaves the engine hot but undamaged, one left to burn boils it over). Fire on the body heats the coolant, 180 degrees an hour
      at full size (authored), and cooks the radiator and its hoses, half the radiator's health an hour (authored): the radiator is
      what fails first in a vehicle fire. A steady heat alone could not meet the owner's rule, since the radiator settles any heat
      within the hour; the failing radiator is what makes a long fire worse than a short one. Put out after an hour, the coolant
      peaks near 102 degrees C with the radiator at half health; after two it has boiled over (FireFigureTests).
    - A block heater (Framework 0.70.0; the owner's idea, 10 Nov 2026): an electric element in the engine, fitted by a mechanic, that
      keeps the coolant at the night heater's 40 degrees C whenever the vehicle is parked, engine off, beside a powered grid (the same
      shore power that trickle-charges the battery, and free from the grid, the same labelled simplification). Real ones plug into
      mains power where winters are cold (secondary: Wikipedia, "Block heater"). Both heaters have a thermostat: full below 35, off at
      40, so neither cools a hot engine just parked.
  -->
  <Phobos.VehicleSystems.PhobosVehicleSystemDef>
    <defName>PhobosVehicleSystem_Cooling</defName>
    <label>cooling system</label>
    <description>The coolant, the thermostat and the radiator. An engine runs warm and steady while they work; with a holed radiator, low coolant or hard work in the heat it runs hot, overheats and boils over, and the engine takes damage. A night heater at camp keeps the coolant warm, so the engine starts easily in the morning.</description>
    <roles>
      <li>Engine</li>
      <li>Radiator</li>
      <li>WaterPump</li>
    </roles>
    <parameters>
      <heatAtFullLoad>420</heatAtFullLoad>
      <radiatorRate>6</radiatorRate>
      <soakRate>0.6</soakRate>
      <thermostatOpens>88</thermostatOpens>
      <engineHeaterTarget>40</engineHeaterTarget>
      <engineHeaterRate>2</engineHeaterRate>
      <!-- The same heater as the cab's (Systems_Cabin.xml, nightHeaterFuelPerHour): change both together. -->
      <nightHeaterFuelPerHour>0.4</nightHeaterFuelPerHour>
      <fireHeat>180</fireHeat>
      <!-- 1 until a block heater is fitted, whose fitting multiplies it to 0 (Fittings.xml), as a fusible plug's figure does. -->
      <blockHeaterAbsent>1</blockHeaterAbsent>
    </parameters>
    <quantities>
      <li>
        <name>coolantTemperature</name>
        <description>The engine's heat, carried to the radiator. It needs coolant, a water pump, a belt and a sound radiator. Cold, the engine wears a little; boiling over, it is damaged.</description>
        <telltale>Phobos/VehicleSystems/Dash/CoolantHot</telltale>
        <showOnDashboard>true</showOnDashboard>
        <label>coolant</label>
        <unit>Celsius</unit>
        <min>-60</min>
        <max>200</max>
        <startsAt>Ambient</startsAt>
        <stages>
          <li>
            <to>40</to>
            <label>cold</label>
            <effects>
              <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Effect_DamagePart">
                <role>Engine</role>
                <perHour>0.15</perHour>
                <when Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.When_EngineRunning" />
              </li>
            </effects>
          </li>
          <li>
            <from>40</from>
            <to>110</to>
            <label>warm</label>
          </li>
          <li>
            <from>110</from>
            <to>120</to>
            <label>running hot</label>
            <notable>true</notable>
            <effects>
              <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Effect_Fleck">
                <fleck>Smoke</fleck>
                <perHour>40</perHour>
                <scale>0.8</scale>
              </li>
              <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Effect_Message">
                <message>PhobosVehicleSystems_MsgRunningHot</message>
                <type>CautionInput</type>
              </li>
            </effects>
          </li>
          <li>
            <from>120</from>
            <to>130</to>
            <label>overheating</label>
            <notable>true</notable>
            <effects>
              <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Effect_DamagePart">
                <role>Engine</role>
                <perHour>3</perHour>
              </li>
              <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Effect_Fleck">
                <fleck>Smoke</fleck>
                <perHour>80</perHour>
                <scale>1.1</scale>
              </li>
              <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Effect_Message">
                <message>PhobosVehicleSystems_MsgOverheating</message>
              </li>
            </effects>
          </li>
          <li>
            <from>130</from>
            <label>boiling over</label>
            <notable>true</notable>
            <effects>
              <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Effect_AddCondition">
                <!-- Framework 0.29.0: an hour of boiling over leaves the head slightly warped, two and a half badly (authored). -->
                <condition>PhobosVehicleCondition_WarpedHead</condition>
                <role>Engine</role>
                <perHour>0.4</perHour>
              </li>
              <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Effect_DamagePart">
                <role>Engine</role>
                <!-- A share of the engine's health, so every engine dies in about the same time (authored, 9 Nov 2026: about 7 hours). -->
                <sharePerHour>0.15</sharePerHour>
              </li>
              <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Effect_RateQuantity">
                <quantity>coolantLevel</quantity>
                <perHour>-0.5</perHour>
              </li>
              <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Effect_Fleck">
                <fleck>Smoke</fleck>
                <perHour>140</perHour>
                <scale>1.4</scale>
              </li>
              <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Effect_SoundLoop">
                <sound>PhobosVehicleSystems_CoolantBoiling</sound>
              </li>
              <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Effect_Letter">
                <label>PhobosVehicleSystems_LetterBoilingLabel</label>
                <text>PhobosVehicleSystems_LetterBoilingText</text>
              </li>
            </effects>
          </li>
        </stages>
      </li>
      <li>
        <name>coolantLevel</name>
        <description>How full the cooling system is. With less coolant the engine runs hotter. Boiling over loses it.</description>
        <telltale>Phobos/VehicleSystems/Dash/CoolantLevel</telltale>
        <label>coolant level</label>
        <unit>Fraction</unit>
        <min>0</min>
        <max>1</max>
        <startsAt>Full</startsAt>
        <stages>
          <li>
            <to>0.6</to>
            <label>low</label>
            <notable>true</notable>
            <effects>
              <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Effect_Message">
                <message>PhobosVehicleSystems_MsgCoolantLow</message>
                <type>CautionInput</type>
              </li>
            </effects>
          </li>
        </stages>
      </li>
      <li>
        <!-- How hard the engine works, for its room heat. -->
        <name>engineLoad</name>
        <description>How hard the engine is working, from idling to flat out. More load makes more heat.</description>
        <label>engine load</label>
        <unit>Fraction</unit>
        <hidden>true</hidden>
        <derivedFrom>
          <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Input_EngineLoad" />
        </derivedFrom>
        <stages>
          <li>
            <from>0.01</from>
            <label>running</label>
            <effects>
              <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Effect_PushHeat">
                <heatPerSecond>12</heatPerSecond>
                <scaleBy Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Input_EngineLoad" />
              </li>
            </effects>
          </li>
        </stages>
      </li>
      <li>
        <!-- Crosslink 4: whether the vehicle is on fire, and how fiercely; while it burns, the radiator and its hoses cook. -->
        <name>bodyFire</name>
        <description>How much of the vehicle is burning. Fire heats everything aboard and ruins the radiator.</description>
        <label>fire</label>
        <unit>Fraction</unit>
        <min>0</min>
        <max>1</max>
        <hidden>true</hidden>
        <derivedFrom>
          <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Input_Fire" />
        </derivedFrom>
        <stages>
          <li>
            <from>0.01</from>
            <label>burning</label>
            <notable>true</notable>
            <effects>
              <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Effect_DamagePart">
                <role>Radiator</role>
                <sharePerHour>0.5</sharePerHour>
              </li>
            </effects>
          </li>
        </stages>
      </li>
      <li>
        <!-- The night heater's fuel on a vehicle whose cab system does not burn it (an open vehicle, or the cab system switched off):
             running, and the cab's own "night heater running" not above a half. -->
        <name>engineHeaterBurning</name>
        <description>A small burner that keeps the engine warm with it off, for an easy start in the cold. It burns a little fuel.</description>
        <label>night heater</label>
        <unit>OnOff</unit>
        <min>0</min>
        <max>1</max>
        <hidden>true</hidden>
        <derivedFrom>
          <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Input_Condition">
            <condition Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.When_All">
              <conditions>
                <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.When_LoadActive"><load>nightHeater</load></li>
                <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.When_QuantityAbove">
                  <quantity>nightHeaterRunning</quantity>
                  <value>0.5</value>
                  <invert>true</invert>
                </li>
              </conditions>
            </condition>
          </li>
        </derivedFrom>
        <stages>
          <li>
            <from>0.5</from>
            <label>burning</label>
            <effects>
              <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Effect_ConsumeFuel">
                <perHourParameter>nightHeaterFuelPerHour</perHourParameter>
              </li>
            </effects>
          </li>
        </stages>
      </li>
    </quantities>
    <flows>
      <li>
        <!-- Heat from the fuel burned, rising steeply as coolant is lost (less coolant to carry it). -->
        <name>combustion</name>
        <quantity>coolantTemperature</quantity>
        <kind>Into</kind>
        <factorParameter>heatAtFullLoad</factorParameter>
        <inputs>
          <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Input_EngineHeat" />
          <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Input_PartHealth">
            <!-- Crosslink 3: a worn engine sheds more heat into its coolant, a fifth more at 40% health and below (authored; see the
                 comment at the top for why not more). -->
            <role>Engine</role>
            <curve>
              <points>
                <li>(0.4, 1.2)</li>
                <li>(1, 1)</li>
              </points>
            </curve>
          </li>
          <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Input_Quantity">
            <quantity>coolantLevel</quantity>
            <curve>
              <points>
                <li>(0, 2.5)</li>
                <li>(0.5, 1.4)</li>
                <li>(1, 1)</li>
              </points>
            </curve>
          </li>
        </inputs>
      </li>
      <li>
        <!-- The radiator, through the thermostat: as good as the radiator part, better on the move, useless without coolant. -->
        <name>radiator</name>
        <quantity>coolantTemperature</quantity>
        <kind>Toward</kind>
        <target Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Input_Ambient" />
        <factorParameter>radiatorRate</factorParameter>
        <openAboveParameter>thermostatOpens</openAboveParameter>
        <band>8</band>
        <inputs>
          <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Input_PartEfficiency">
            <role>Radiator</role>
          </li>
          <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Input_PartEfficiency">
            <!-- A radiator cools only the coolant brought to it. A vehicle with no water pump of its own reads as sound. -->
            <role>WaterPump</role>
          </li>
          <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Input_PartEfficiency">
            <!-- Framework 0.73.0: the drive belt turns the water pump, and on most engines the radiator's fan with it. None reads as sound. -->
            <role>Belt</role>
          </li>
          <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Input_Moving">
            <stopped>0.7</stopped>
            <moving>1</moving>
          </li>
          <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Input_EngineRpm">
            <curve>
              <points>
                <li>(0, 0.6)</li>
                <li>(700, 0.75)</li>
                <li>(1500, 1)</li>
              </points>
            </curve>
          </li>
          <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Input_Quantity">
            <quantity>coolantLevel</quantity>
            <curve>
              <points>
                <li>(0, 0.05)</li>
                <li>(1, 1)</li>
              </points>
            </curve>
          </li>
        </inputs>
      </li>
      <li>
        <name>soak</name>
        <quantity>coolantTemperature</quantity>
        <kind>Toward</kind>
        <target Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Input_Ambient" />
        <factorParameter>soakRate</factorParameter>
      </li>
      <li>
        <!-- Crosslink 1: the night heater at camp keeps the coolant warm (see the comment at the top). -->
        <name>nightHeater</name>
        <quantity>coolantTemperature</quantity>
        <kind>Toward</kind>
        <target Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Input_Parameter"><parameter>engineHeaterTarget</parameter></target>
        <factorParameter>engineHeaterRate</factorParameter>
        <!-- Its thermostat (Framework 0.70.0): full below 35 degrees C, off at 40, so it never cools a hot engine just parked. -->
        <openBelowParameter>engineHeaterTarget</openBelowParameter>
        <band>5</band>
        <when Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.When_LoadActive"><load>nightHeater</load></when>
      </li>
      <li>
        <!-- The block heater fitting (Framework 0.70.0, see the comment at the top): from the colony's power while parked, engine off. -->
        <name>blockHeater</name>
        <quantity>coolantTemperature</quantity>
        <kind>Toward</kind>
        <target Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Input_Parameter"><parameter>engineHeaterTarget</parameter></target>
        <factorParameter>engineHeaterRate</factorParameter>
        <openBelowParameter>engineHeaterTarget</openBelowParameter>
        <band>5</band>
        <inputs>
          <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Input_ShorePower" />
        </inputs>
        <when Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.When_All">
          <conditions>
            <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.When_ParameterBelow"><parameter>blockHeaterAbsent</parameter><value>0.5</value></li>
            <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.When_EngineRunning"><invert>true</invert></li>
          </conditions>
        </when>
      </li>
      <li>
        <!-- Crosslink 4: fire on the body heats the coolant (see the comment at the top). -->
        <name>fire</name>
        <quantity>coolantTemperature</quantity>
        <kind>Into</kind>
        <factorParameter>fireHeat</factorParameter>
        <inputs>
          <li Class="Phobos.VehicleSystems.Input_Fire" />
        </inputs>
      </li>
    </flows>
    <!-- The cold start's wear moved to the oil's temperature in Framework 0.70.0 (crosslink 7; Systems_Lubrication.xml). -->
    <service>
      <label>a coolant top-up</label>
      <dueQuantity>coolantLevel</dueQuantity>
      <dueBelow>0.6</dueBelow>
      <restores>
        <li>
          <quantity>coolantLevel</quantity>
          <value>1</value>
        </li>
      </restores>
      <suppliesWithoutWaterSource>
        <Chemfuel>3</Chemfuel>
      </suppliesWithoutWaterSource>
      <waterLitres>10</waterLitres>
      <work>300</work>
    </service>
    <onPartHit>
      <li>
        <role>WaterPump</role>
        <quantity>coolantLevel</quantity>
        <perShareLost>-0.25</perShareLost>
      </li>
      <li>
        <!-- Shares of the part's health, so a small radiator and a big one agree (authored, 9 Nov 2026, after the owner's test: a wrecked radiator used to
             cost at most 45% of the coolant). -->
        <role>Radiator</role>
        <quantity>coolantLevel</quantity>
        <perShareLost>-0.9</perShareLost>
      </li>
      <li>
        <role>Engine</role>
        <quantity>coolantLevel</quantity>
        <perShareLost>-0.3</perShareLost>
      </li>
      <li>
        <!-- Framework 0.29.0: the hole goes on leaking (Conditions.xml). A third of a radiator's health shot away is leaking (0.3). Authored. -->
        <role>Radiator</role>
        <condition>PhobosVehicleCondition_HoledRadiator</condition>
        <perShareLost>0.9</perShareLost>
        <armorCategories>
          <li>Sharp</li>
          <li>Blunt</li>
        </armorCategories>
        <!-- Not an animal's scratch or bite, and not the scratch Vehicle Framework's hand brakes give wheels. -->
        <exceptDamageDefs>
          <li>Scratch</li>
          <li>Bite</li>
        </exceptDamageDefs>
      </li>
    </onPartHit>
  </Phobos.VehicleSystems.PhobosVehicleSystemDef>
</Defs>
